  "recipe": {
    "technique": "Oshima Tsumugi kimono are produced in a lengthy process with over 30 steps. To prepare for the shimebata binding stage, 16–20 threads of about 16 meters in length, are grouped together, stretched between two posts, and covered in Igisu, a jelly-like paste made from seaweed. The threads must dry perfectly round for the shimebata stage, in order to create sharp kasuri. Shimebata is the technique used to make the kasuri threads. The white silk threads are tightly woven with cotton threads creating a dense mat. Areas of the silk threads are exposed according to the design. When dyed only certain of the areas of the thread will take color."
